1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,090
This is the Great Salt Lake in Utah,

2
00:00:02,252 --> 00:00:04,574
the largest saline lake
in the Western Hemisphere.

3
00:00:04,841 --> 00:00:09,718
In June 1985, its surface
measured 4,209.7 feet.

4
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:14,269
In July 2022, it measured 4,190.2 feet.

5
00:00:14,582 --> 00:00:19,737
The lake's record high had come
in 1986, at 4,211.6 feet.

6
00:00:20,051 --> 00:00:21,955
Now it sat more than 20 feet lower.

7
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:24,869
The Landsat satellites
were watching the whole time.

8
00:00:25,148 --> 00:00:28,921
Put the pictures in order,
from 1985 to 2022,

9
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:31,231
and the lake pulls back from its shore.

10
00:00:31,556 --> 00:00:34,540
These are real Landsat
scenes from the U.S.

11
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:36,177
Geological Survey.

12
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:41,146
Each picture is a mosaic of several
scenes, shown with one color curve,

13
00:00:41,297 --> 00:00:44,060
so the colors can be compared
from year to year.

14
00:00:44,432 --> 00:00:48,994
It is a big lake: about 70
miles long, 30 miles wide,

15
00:00:49,169 --> 00:00:53,929
and about 40 feet deep, and up
to 8 times as salty as seawater.

16
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:58,875
At about 4,200 feet, it covers
roughly 1,700 square miles.

17
00:00:59,177 --> 00:01:02,892
At its 1986 high, it covered about 2,300.

18
00:01:03,147 --> 00:01:06,456
It is a terminal lake: there
is no outlet to the sea.

19
00:01:06,758 --> 00:01:08,430
And it matters to the economy.

20
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:13,887
USGS says the lake generates nearly
$200 million a year from recreation

21
00:01:14,119 --> 00:01:17,637
and the brine shrimp harvest,
citing a 2012 study.

22
00:01:18,113 --> 00:01:23,697
In July 2021, the gauge
at Saltair read 4,191.3

23
00:01:23,790 --> 00:01:27,946
feet, the lowest mark since
measurements began in 1875.

24
00:01:28,504 --> 00:01:33,519
NASA reports that at that level
the lake covered about 950 square miles,

25
00:01:33,716 --> 00:01:37,327
44 percent less surface
than at 4,200 feet.

26
00:01:37,687 --> 00:01:40,172
A year later, it fell lower still.

27
00:01:40,485 --> 00:01:44,676
NASA lists a new record
low on July 3, 2022.

28
00:01:45,036 --> 00:01:46,197
Why did it fall?

29
00:01:46,394 --> 00:01:49,517
NASA's account points
to people, and to climate.

30
00:01:50,330 --> 00:01:55,229
For about a century, water has been diverted
upstream for homes and farms.

31
00:01:55,926 --> 00:01:59,386
Utah State University
researchers estimate that inflows

32
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:03,391
have fallen 39 percent since 1850.

33
00:02:03,391 --> 00:02:08,708
A 2016 white paper cited by NASA found
that the lake's volume has shrunk 48 percent,

34
00:02:08,871 --> 00:02:12,691
and that its level
has fallen 11 feet since 1847.

35
00:02:13,109 --> 00:02:17,405
Meanwhile, the Salt Lake City
metro area grew from 700,000 people

36
00:02:17,463 --> 00:02:21,665
in 1982 to 1.2 million in 2022.

37
00:02:22,362 --> 00:02:23,953
And the climate is changing.

38
00:02:24,231 --> 00:02:27,192
More precipitation falls
as rain than snow,

39
00:02:27,366 --> 00:02:30,222
and the snowpack releases
its water more slowly.

40
00:02:30,663 --> 00:02:36,596
In 1959, a solid-fill railroad causeway
was completed across the middle of the lake.

41
00:02:36,921 --> 00:02:41,774
The causeway took 50 million cubic
yards of rock, sand and gravel.

42
00:02:42,053 --> 00:02:46,407
It replaced a 12-mile trestle
of 28,000 wooden pilings,

43
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:50,923
and it had only two 15-foot culverts
for water to pass through.

44
00:02:51,434 --> 00:02:56,786
It divides the lake into Gunnison Bay
in the north, and Gilbert Bay in the south.

45
00:02:57,297 --> 00:03:00,629
Almost all of the fresh water
flows in on the south side,

46
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:02,928
so the north is always saltier.

47
00:03:03,369 --> 00:03:09,314
In July 1984, the south side stood
3.7 feet higher than the north,

48
00:03:09,488 --> 00:03:11,764
the largest difference ever recorded.

49
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:17,836
That August, a 300-foot section
of the causeway was replaced by a low bridge.

50
00:03:18,161 --> 00:03:21,829
A new channel opened in December 2016.

51
00:03:22,155 --> 00:03:24,709
The divide had a strange side effect.

52
00:03:25,092 --> 00:03:30,549
Dense brine from Gunnison Bay sank into a deep
layer near the bottom of Gilbert Bay.

53
00:03:30,874 --> 00:03:35,820
USGS says that layer had low
pH and no dissolved oxygen,

54
00:03:36,029 --> 00:03:41,091
and conditions that favored turning
inorganic mercury into methyl mercury,

55
00:03:41,265 --> 00:03:43,285
which builds up in the food chain.

56
00:03:43,668 --> 00:03:48,428
It dissipated by June 2014,
after its source water was cut off.

57
00:03:49,009 --> 00:03:51,029
The lake has not always been too low.

58
00:03:51,447 --> 00:03:56,811
After rainy weather beginning
in 1982, it peaked in June 1986,

59
00:03:57,043 --> 00:04:00,212
and again in March and April of 1987.

60
00:04:00,584 --> 00:04:04,903
Then came a pumping project,
the Newfoundland Evaporation Basin.

61
00:04:05,599 --> 00:04:09,001
The project built almost
25 miles of dikes,

62
00:04:09,117 --> 00:04:14,026
and about 1.5 million acre-feet
were pumped in the first year.

63
00:04:14,026 --> 00:04:18,496
The pumps ran from April
1987 to June 1989,

64
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:21,108
and the lake dropped almost 6 feet.

65
00:04:21,491 --> 00:04:25,241
USGS says the pumping caused
about 2 feet of that.

66
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,585
When the water pulls back,
it leaves lake bed.

67
00:04:29,026 --> 00:04:34,622
NASA scientist Dorothy Hall says
exposed lake beds often harbor toxic

68
00:04:34,715 --> 00:04:38,825
minerals from agricultural runoff,
and NASA reports that dust

69
00:04:38,883 --> 00:04:43,143
from the drying Great Salt Lake
worsens regional air pollution.

70
00:04:43,654 --> 00:04:49,123
USGS collected dust at 17 sites
in northern Utah, near the lake bed,

71
00:04:49,367 --> 00:04:52,258
especially at Farmington
and Bear River Bays,

72
00:04:52,467 --> 00:04:56,809
and measured pollutants including
arsenic, lead and cadmium.

73
00:04:57,412 --> 00:04:59,142
Its conclusion is careful.

74
00:04:59,526 --> 00:05:02,928
The dust may contribute to potential
health risks for children,

75
00:05:03,171 --> 00:05:08,895
but USGS says no formal evaluation of its impact
on human health has been conducted,

76
00:05:09,139 --> 00:05:10,532
because of data gaps.

77
00:05:11,043 --> 00:05:14,735
Then came the winter of 2022 to 2023,

78
00:05:14,886 --> 00:05:17,336
with record snowfall across the region.

79
00:05:17,777 --> 00:05:22,746
NASA's Earth Observatory shows more snow
than usual around the lake that February.

80
00:05:23,558 --> 00:05:25,172
Did that fix the problem?

81
00:05:25,869 --> 00:05:30,176
NASA's GRACE satellites measure
underground water across the Great Basin,

82
00:05:30,385 --> 00:05:32,521
a region of the western United States.

83
00:05:32,846 --> 00:05:37,677
They show it fell by 16.5 cubic
miles over about two decades.

84
00:05:38,118 --> 00:05:44,039
Scientist Dorothy Hall expected the record
snow to help, but she said, overall,

85
00:05:44,213 --> 00:05:45,699
the decline continued.

86
00:05:46,210 --> 00:05:47,754
What do these pictures show?

87
00:05:48,079 --> 00:05:50,715
Where the shoreline
was on particular days.

88
00:05:50,958 --> 00:05:53,628
They do not forecast what comes next.

89
00:05:54,011 --> 00:05:57,634
But the measurements
are plain: in July 2022,

90
00:05:57,785 --> 00:06:02,719
the Great Salt Lake measured 4,190.2 feet,

91
00:06:02,928 --> 00:06:06,179
more than 20 feet below its 1986 high.

92
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:11,055
And nearly four decades of Landsat
show where that water used to be.
